WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.037
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:08.700
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:16.076
Unit twenty: Stylistics. Today you will analyse style precisely with complex noun phrases and prepositions.

4
00:00:16.376 --> 00:00:19.063
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.210
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:24.958
Style.

7
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.844
Style means the distinctive way a writer uses language.

8
00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:32.100
Hemingway's style is famously spare.

9
00:00:32.683 --> 00:00:35.394
Your turn. Say: Style.

10
00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:39.385
Style.

11
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:40.989
Diction.

12
00:00:41.983 --> 00:00:44.447
Diction means the writer's choice of words.

13
00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:47.885
Archaic diction gives the poem a timeless feel.

14
00:00:48.483 --> 00:00:50.822
Your turn. Say: Diction.

15
00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:54.726
Diction.

16
00:00:55.483 --> 00:00:56.473
Syntax.

17
00:00:57.483 --> 00:01:00.655
Syntax means the arrangement of words in sentences.

18
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:04.074
Fragmented syntax mirrors the speaker's panic.

19
00:01:04.683 --> 00:01:07.220
Your turn. Say: Syntax.

20
00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:11.172
Syntax.

21
00:01:11.917 --> 00:01:12.674
Tone.

22
00:01:13.683 --> 00:01:16.136
Tone means the attitude conveyed by the writing.

23
00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.819
The tone is detached and ironic.

24
00:01:19.417 --> 00:01:21.744
Your turn. Say: Tone.

25
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.253
Tone.

26
00:01:26.017 --> 00:01:26.925
Imagery.

27
00:01:27.917 --> 00:01:30.961
Imagery means language that appeals to the senses.

28
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.664
Vivid imagery recreates the market's noise and smells.

29
00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.960
Your turn. Say: Imagery.

30
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:41.697
Imagery.

31
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:43.323
Register.

32
00:01:44.317 --> 00:01:46.583
Register means the level of formality.

33
00:01:47.183 --> 00:01:49.961
A sudden shift in register surprises the reader.

34
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.005
Your turn. Say: Register.

35
00:01:55.867 --> 00:01:56.903
Register.

36
00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:58.639
Foregrounding.

37
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:03.008
Foregrounding means making certain language stand out from the norm.

38
00:02:03.617 --> 00:02:06.893
Repetition is a common foregrounding device.

39
00:02:07.483 --> 00:02:10.461
Your turn. Say: Foregrounding.

40
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:14.382
Foregrounding.

41
00:02:15.117 --> 00:02:15.955
Effect.

42
00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.785
Effect means the impact a technique has on the reader.

43
00:02:20.383 --> 00:02:22.278
The effect of the short line is shock.

44
00:02:22.883 --> 00:02:25.466
Your turn. Say: Effect.

45
00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:29.046
Effect.

46
00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:33.612
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:38.797
Visal, you analysed the opening of the war novel. What stood out stylistically?

48
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:46.921
The use of short sentences creates a sense of urgency. 'He ran. The bridge fell. No one spoke.'

49
00:02:48.017 --> 00:02:50.585
And how does that compare with the second chapter?

50
00:02:51.683 --> 00:02:59.630
There's a dramatic shift in syntax. The long, flowing sentences with their many clauses suggest a return to calm and memory.

51
00:03:00.717 --> 00:03:02.705
Did the diction support that change?

52
00:03:03.817 --> 00:03:11.566
Yes. The replacement of harsh, monosyllabic verbs with softer, descriptive adjectives slows the reader down.

53
00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.189
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:18.547
Grammar focus: Noun phrases with prepositions.

55
00:03:18.847 --> 00:03:20.104
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:20.817 --> 00:03:24.024
The use of short sentences creates a sense of urgency.

57
00:03:24.917 --> 00:03:28.646
A dramatic shift in syntax suggests a return to calm.

58
00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:33.721
The replacement of harsh verbs with soft adjectives slows the reader down.

59
00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:45.829
In stylistic analysis, the head noun is followed by a fixed preposition: the use OF, a shift IN, the contrast BETWEEN, the replacement OF … WITH. The verb agrees with the head noun, not the last noun.

60
00:03:46.583 --> 00:03:51.279
Be careful! Don't say: The use of short sentences create urgency.

61
00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:56.893
Say: The use of short sentences creates urgency.

62
00:03:57.193 --> 00:03:59.065
The head noun 'use' is singular.

63
00:03:59.867 --> 00:04:02.368
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:04.853
How Style Makes Meaning.

65
00:04:05.153 --> 00:04:12.864
Stylistics bridges linguistics and literary criticism by examining how specific language choices produce effects.

66
00:04:13.867 --> 00:04:19.988
Rather than asking simply what a text means, the stylistician asks how particular features create that meaning.

67
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:27.086
The repetition of a single word, for instance, may foreground an idea that the reader would otherwise overlook.

68
00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:34.395
Similarly, a sudden shift in register can signal irony, while dense imagery may slow the pace of reading.

69
00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:41.033
A convincing stylistic analysis therefore links every observation to a demonstrable effect on the reader.

70
00:04:42.133 --> 00:04:46.123
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:46.983 --> 00:04:49.424
The use of long sentences blank the pace.

72
00:04:49.724 --> 00:04:53.157
A, slows. B, slow. C, are slowing.

73
00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.665
The answer is A: slows.

74
00:04:59.817 --> 00:05:04.080
Choose the correct preposition: a sudden shift blank tone.

75
00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.383
A, of. B, in. C, at.

76
00:05:10.933 --> 00:05:12.921
The answer is B: in.

77
00:05:14.083 --> 00:05:16.547
Language that appeals to the senses is blank.

78
00:05:16.847 --> 00:05:20.245
A, syntax. B, imagery. C, diction.

79
00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:25.985
The answer is B: imagery.

80
00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:30.206
Making certain language stand out from the norm is blank.

81
00:05:30.506 --> 00:05:34.218
A, register. B, effect. C, foregrounding.

82
00:05:37.767 --> 00:05:39.743
The answer is C: foregrounding.

83
00:05:40.883 --> 00:05:44.857
According to the reading, a good analysis links observations to…

84
00:05:45.157 --> 00:05:50.296
A, the author's life. B, a demonstrable effect. C, the book's sales.

85
00:05:53.833 --> 00:05:56.738
The answer is B: a demonstrable effect.

86
00:05:57.933 --> 00:06:01.050
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:01.917 --> 00:06:04.833
How does sentence structure affect the tone of a text?

88
00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:23.243
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:23.543 --> 00:06:59.086
Sentence structure strongly affects tone because the rhythm of a sentence shapes how the reader feels. The use of short, simple sentences tends to create tension or urgency, as in action scenes. By contrast, long sentences with many clauses can produce a reflective or nostalgic tone. For example, a sudden one-word sentence after a long paragraph often shocks the reader. Some argue that vocabulary matters more than structure. In my view, the two always work together.

90
00:06:59.386 --> 00:07:01.106
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:02.017 --> 00:07:06.689
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and noun phrases with prepositions.

92
00:07:06.989 --> 00:07:10.895
Today's value is precision. Choose your words and numbers with care.

93
00:07:11.195 --> 00:07:14.045
See you in Unit twenty-one. Goodbye!

94
00:07:14.345 --> 00:07:17.659
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

